{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n37 \n \n \nB. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies \n1. Mission anomalies  \nThere were no significant anomalies hindering Flame Design operations during \nIncrements 59-60, but there was a pertinent anomaly that had previously occurred.  \nIt was discovered on Feb. 4 (GMT 035) that the pressure was low in a check gas bottle \nfor the CIR\u2019s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP).  The bottle had \nbeen on the space station for 9 years, and the low pressure was attributed to a \nnominal leak rate.  The check gas was for calibration of the GCIP measurements and \nnot needed for regular operations which proceeded unhindered.  The bottle was \nreplaced on June 13 to enable the necessary calibration. \n \n2. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data   \nFlame Design tests sometimes automatically aborted (due to the controlling software \nscript), where that was typically prior to gas flow and the activation of the igniter.  As \na result, there was little lost other than the time in the failed test attempt.   Such \naborts normally occur because of communication issues with an Image Processing and \nStorage Unit (IPSU).  These are infrequent, but there are sometimes days when ACME \ntests have aborted repeatedly.  \n \nACME tests are of course also unsuccessful when the flame fails to ignite.  The first \ntest of the day has sometimes failed to ignite, presumably because the fuel line has \nnot been sufficiently primed.  Ignition was sometimes unsuccessful because the \nsettings for the automatic flame detection are such that detection threshold is \nreached because of the igniter glow rather than a flame.  As a result of the detection, \nthe hot-wire igniter is deactivated and retracted, which is of course an issue if the \nflame has yet to ignite.  This is typically avoided by detecting ignition with \nmeasurement of the flame\u2019s OH* chemiluminescence with a Photomultiplier Tube \n(PMT) module.  The OH* chemiluminescence, in the ultraviolet at 310 nm, is a good \nindicator of an ignited flame rather than the glowing igniter, which emits primarily in \nthe infrared and visible spectrum.  However, the combination of igniter settings and \nPMT gains can lead to false ignition detection. \n \nC. Completeness/Quality of Data \nThe number of flames ignited in the round 1 testing significantly exceeds the normal-\nflame tests which were specified as required in the ACME Integrated Science", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 37}}